00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
the european union's main institutions  have reached a final agreement on  sweeping reforms to the eu's common  agricultural policy or cap  paving the way for the package to enter  force in january 2015.  the agreement between the council the  european parliament and the commission  covers specific financial aspects of the  reform  for example direct subsidy payments to  certain farmers would be adjusted  and increased for small farmholders in  addition the eu will boost spending on  rural development projects in less  developed regions  the key point of the reform is to  introduce compulsory greening measures  in the cap  to help make the agricultural sector  more sustainable  farmers would also be eligible for set  up grants  the cap aims to maintain living  standards for farmers while ensuring  secure food supplies  at fair prices for consumers spending on  agriculture accounts for more than 30  percent  of the eu annual budget at the moment  while it has been progressively  decreasing in size  efforts to change the policy have proven  difficult  talks on these reforms have dragged on  for a year and a half the economic  downturn  and in particular the need to finalize  the eu's next long-term budget  have made agreement on these issues even  more complex  the deal came after eu farm ministers  reached a common position on the reforms  in talks on the 23rd of september  the package must now be formally adopted  by the council and the parliament  which should be a formality
 

